WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.503 --> 00:00:07.920
DAVE SMITH: My
name is Dave Smith.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.587
I'm a developer
advocate here at Google,

00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:11.430
working on IoT platforms.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.920
And today, I'd like to spend
a little bit of time talking

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.170
to you about how
you can build Cloud

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.450
services for smart
devices, and how

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.960
you can integrate those services
with the Google Assistant.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.690
We're going to walk through some
components of an example Cloud

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.450
service solution, and then
we'll discuss a little bit

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.180
of how you can connect
a service like that

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.300
to the Assistant for smart
home device interactions.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.310
All right, so let's get started.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:39.300
So voice assistants have
accelerated adoption

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.160
of smart devices in the home.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.140
They've become the
common thread that

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.200
enables different manufacturers
to work seamlessly

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.990
together inside of one space.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.660
The Smart Home API
enables developers

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.000
to integrate their
Cloud-connected devices

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.540
with the Google Assistant, and
leverage the natural language

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.350
processing that's built into
the Assistant for processing

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.630
queries and commands for those
devices that are in the home.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.950
Smart Home is part
of Actions on Google,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.680
which is the wider
platform designed

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.980
to enable conversational
engagements with users

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.600
through assistant-enabled
devices.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.970
However, Smart Home uses a more
simplified model of interaction

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.570
that abstracts away some of
the conversational pieces

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.197
of a typical voice action.

00:01:25.197 --> 00:01:26.780
Just out of curiosity,
how many of you

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.780
have developed using the
Actions on Google Cloud

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.050
Platform before?

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.760
OK, so for those
of you who have,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.260
you're familiar with some of
the conversational interactions

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.512
that typically take place
when building an action.

00:01:36.512 --> 00:01:38.970
And using Smart Home, you're
going to see that some of that

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.260
is simplified quite a bit.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.000
Developers don't
really have to interact

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.020
with the request and response
model of talking to the user.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.870
We focus more on the devices.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.490
So with Smart Home
integrations, we

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.640
focused on creating a clear
and structured interface

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.910
for devices.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.830
Devices are broken into
device types and traits, which

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.860
provide a well-understood schema
for reporting those device

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.900
capabilities to Google.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.600
You can see an example of
two of those device types

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.780
here on the slide, the light
device and a thermostat device,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.580
and we have many others as well.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.920
Within these individual
device types,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.750
these encapsulate
a series of, what

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.200
we call traits that define
what the capabilities are

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.560
of that device.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.560
So, for example, with
the light, that device

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.010
can be turned on and off.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.820
You can adjust the brightness
or you can, in some cases,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.680
maybe, modify the color setting.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.050
Whereas for a thermostat,
you have the capability

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.710
of modifying what we call
the temperature setting.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.290
And then within each
individual trait,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.900
these are further
subdivided to provide

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.170
individual attributes
that describe the state

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.710
of that particular device.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.440
So, for example,
with a thermostat,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.990
the temperature
setting trait defines

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.170
three attributes for that
thermostat-- mode, like heat

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.100
or cool or off or auto, things
like that, the temperature set

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.080
point, as well as the
ambient temperature.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.840
So we put all these
things together

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.090
to define what we call
the device schema,

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:07.300
to provide known device types
interactions with the Google

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.850
Assistant.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.430
Now, having such a
well-defined schema

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.630
enables the Assistant to bring
highly intelligent interactions

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.360
about how they process
queries and commands,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.300
because it now has a strong
understanding of what

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.970
those devices are and
what those devices can do.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.660
In other words,
with Smart Home, we

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.800
allow Google to handle
the conversation

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.800
so that the developers
can focus on the devices.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.550
The Google Assistant also
adds contextual understanding

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.650
to your devices
through the Home Graph.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.390
Home Graph is our
technology that

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.410
understands the relationships
between structures, rooms,

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.820
users, and devices.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.880
The Home Graph is what
powers our ability

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.570
to understand user queries
in context of the environment

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.240
and be able to translate that
back to individual devices.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.970
It enables us to
say things like,

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.750
turn on the upstairs lights, or
what's the temperature in here,

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.810
by providing that relative
context between the user

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.270
and the devices around them.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.580
All right, quick show of hands.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.893
How many of you in here own
an Assistant-enabled device

00:04:13.893 --> 00:04:16.310
in your home, whether that's
a Smart Speaker like a Google

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.490
Home or a Smart Display like a
Home Hub that I have up here?

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:20.422
OK, good.

00:04:20.422 --> 00:04:22.880
Now for those of you who raised
your hands, how many of you

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:26.450
have at least one smart device
in your home that is connected

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.120
to that Assistant device?

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.920
OK, good, good, so about
half, maybe a little bit less.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.680
So what I'd like to do here
is I want to quickly show you

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.560
a demo so that everybody's
on the same page of what

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.880
this actually looks
like in practice.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.820
So up here, I have
a Google Home Hub

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.920
and I have a Nest
thermostat, and I'm just

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.160
going to walk through some
very simple interactions of how

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.130
we can interact, as a
user, with the Assistant,

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.670
with one of these smart devices.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.170
So let's quickly
switch to the demo.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.990
GOOGLE ASSISTANT:
The mic's back on.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.470
DAVE SMITH: All right, so
wake up my devices here.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:06.110
All right, so as a user, I've
installed both of these devices

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.530
in my home and I use
the Google Assistant

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.830
to set these devices up, add
them to rooms in my home,

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.080
and do all these pieces
associated with the Home Graph

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.560
that I just described.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.740
And what that enables
me to do as a user is

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.500
walk up to my device and
say simple queries like, hey

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.870
Google, what's the
temperature on stage?

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.960
GOOGLE ASSISTANT: It's
currently 71 degrees.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.980
DAVE SMITH: So this is the
Smart Home API in action, right?

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.050
Basically what's
happening is Google

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.120
is connecting to the
partner's Cloud service,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.730
in this case Nest,
and it's asking

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.760
for the current state of
that particular device

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.070
and then reporting
that back to the user.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.580
Notice, also, that I'm referring
to that device by its location,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.170
in this case, on stage.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.790
And this is because
the Assistant

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.570
has assigned that device to the
room using the Home Graph, when

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.870
I initially set it up.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:00.360
Now because the Home Graph
knows that this display

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.250
and that thermostat are
both in the same room,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:08.400
I can also make more relative
queries like, hey Google,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.930
what's the temperature in here?

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.950
GOOGLE ASSISTANT: It's
currently 71 degrees.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.340
DAVE SMITH: We get the
same result, right?

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.160
Similarly, we can send
commands to these devices

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.630
to make a change.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:23.060
Hey Google, set the
temperature to 65 degrees.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.245
GOOGLE ASSISTANT: OK, setting
the stage to 65 degrees.

00:06:28.245 --> 00:06:30.370
DAVE SMITH: And we can see
the adjustment also made

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.210
on the thermostat as well.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.450
So this is, again, using
that Smart Home API.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.120
But in this case, Google
is sending a command

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.310
to that partner cloud
integration to say,

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.500
you need to adjust the state
of this particular device.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.480
And in this case, it's
providing a new set point, OK?

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.770
Now even here, we can use
Google's natural language

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.200
understanding to make
more natural queries

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.470
at the same time.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:58.822
Hey, Google, make
it a little warmer.

00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:00.030
GOOGLE ASSISTANT: You got it.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.810
Warming up the stage.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:02.820
DAVE SMITH: OK.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.418
Now, the other nice thing
about integrating your devices

00:07:05.418 --> 00:07:06.960
with the Google
Assistant is not only

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.700
do you get this
voice interaction,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.060
but on Assistant surfaces
that have UI, like this Smart

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.880
Display, or an Android
phone, or an IOS device,

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.380
you can also interact
using the touch UI.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.370
So, for example, you saw
it for just a moment,

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:24.200
but I'll bring it
back up quickly.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.160
I can look at the view of
that device on this display

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.530
and I can interact
with it directly

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:35.830
to make that change
on the device as well.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.830
Now, all of this
functionality is

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:42.150
built into the Google Home app,
into Google Assistant devices.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.880
And all you have to provide
is the interaction of how

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.070
to ask your devices
for information

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.932
and how to manipulate
them through commands.

00:07:48.932 --> 00:07:50.640
And we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.360
about how all that stuff works.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.818
So let's switch
back to the slides.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.970
GOOGLE ASSISTANT: The mic's off.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:07:58.887
DAVE SMITH: All right.

00:08:01.173 --> 00:08:03.590
So there's two things that I
want to go over with you here

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:03.890
today.

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:05.432
And the first that
we want to discuss

00:08:05.432 --> 00:08:07.460
is, let's talk a
little bit about,

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.920
how do we actually build
a Cloud service that

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.440
communicates with devices?

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.750
So, quickly, just kind of
showing you a diagram, this

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.300
is essentially what you
just saw in the demo, right?

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.020
The user can interact with
an Assistant-enabled device,

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.470
and that assistive device
will interact with your Cloud

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.560
service in order to communicate
with that particular device.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.690
So the Smart Home interaction
is a cloud-to-cloud integration,

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.950
which means, as a
device manufacturer,

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.789
you have to provide your
own cloud service for users

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.760
to register and
manage their devices.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.950
And then the Smart
Home actions platform

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:46.050
can integrate with
that cloud service

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.080
using a series of web
hooks to control and access

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:49.920
those devices.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.370
We'll talk more about
that in a little bit.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.870
Now, depending on where you are
in your development process,

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.150
you may already have
this functionality

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.640
in place for your
devices, but I want

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.880
to explore a little bit of
what an example might look

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.570
like if you were to build this
device cloud service on Google

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.830
Cloud.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.500
So this is an example
architecture using services

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.813
from the Google Cloud Platform,
as well as from Firebase,

00:09:14.813 --> 00:09:17.230
that we use to fulfill the
requirements for the Smart Home

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:18.170
use case.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.220
Now the sample code for what I'm
going to show you is on GitHub,

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.710
so I recommend that you take
a look at that after the talk.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.740
It's an end to end sample that
includes the Cloud service

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:29.410
as well as client applications
for mobile and web,

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:33.510
interactions with sample
devices, all that stuff.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.250
So in the sample
architecture, we

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.220
have Cloud IoT Core, which
enables secure device

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.300
connectivity over
and MGTT or HTDP.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.900
We're also using Firebase, which
enables easy client application

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.930
development for mobile and
web through their series

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.790
of native investee case.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.550
We also get access to
Firebase authentication,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:54.990
which adds the
ability to provide

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:59.680
user identity and access
control over all of this data.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.460
Now, in this example, devices
report their current state

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.620
through IoT Core, which then
bridges that data into Pub/Sub

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.800
and through to Cloud Firestore.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.620
Using Firebase
authentication in Firestore,

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:18.190
we can build client applications
that expose that data directly

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.520
to users for device management.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.320
And then users now
have the ability

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.690
to control their
devices, and they do that

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.180
by updating the
data in Firestore,

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:31.420
which then triggers
commands back to the devices

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.580
through IoT Core.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:35.860
Now, this means that,
in this architecture,

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:39.040
Firestore becomes the
primary source of truth

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.830
for all of your device data.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.130
And that's going to become
really important when

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:43.755
we start talking more
about Smart Home,

00:10:43.755 --> 00:10:46.000
so I'll come back to that
a little bit later on.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.430
But just keep in mind for
now that Cloud Firestore

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.680
is that central point
where all of the data

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:54.910
is accessible for
all of the devices.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:58.040
OK, so why Cloud IoT Core?

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.650
IoT Core enables
secure connections

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:04.320
to Google Cloud for resource
constrained devices, OK.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.230
Think small, tiny, $1
microcontroller, less than $1

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:10.770
type devices, right?

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.770
Generally speaking, if
you're building something

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:14.210
like a smart light,
you're not going

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:16.085
to want to put a quad
core processor in there

00:11:16.085 --> 00:11:19.710
running embedded Linux, I hope.

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:22.530
Instead, you typically
want low power devices

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:26.230
that have very low footprint,
and all of those things.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:28.470
So we use Cloud
IoT Core to enable

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:31.230
those devices to connect
directly to Google Cloud,

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.770
and that's done two ways.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:37.170
The first is using MQTT, which
is a low overhead protocol that

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:41.430
is ideally suited for IoT
devices or low bandwidth

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.500
devices.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.680
We do also support HTDP, but
generally speaking, most users

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.440
of IoT Core are using the
MQTT protocol, primarily

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:50.880
for this reason.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.220
The other, and probably
more important reason,

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.910
is for authentication.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:57.180
So we need to make sure
that these devices have

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.940
a secure connection
to Google Cloud.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.850
And as I mentioned before,
generally speaking we

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.960
try to get the smallest, lowest
footprint, cheapest device we

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.350
can into these Smart
Home devices, whatever

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.900
we need to supply the use case.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.650
This often introduces
constraints on both CPU

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.682
as well as memory,
primarily memory, right.

00:12:18.682 --> 00:12:20.640
The cheaper the device,
the less onboard memory

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.690
it's going to have.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:26.520
In addition to this, IoT
solutions really, to be secure,

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.730
need a mutual
authentication solution.

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.220
It's not enough for
the device to be

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.590
able to trust the server
that it's connecting to.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.785
It's just as important
for the server

00:12:36.785 --> 00:12:38.910
to be able to trust the
devices that are connecting

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:41.253
to it in an IoT situation, OK.

00:12:41.253 --> 00:12:43.170
So we need to have mutual
authentication going

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.940
between these two pieces.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.550
Generally speaking, you
would solve that problem

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:51.330
with something like a mutual
TLS stack on your device

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.690
or on your client.

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:56.130
However, typical
mutual TLS stacks

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.200
require way too much memory
for a microcontroller

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:02.880
in a lot of cases, whether it's
the stack itself or the fact

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.340
that the client is also
responsible for housing

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:06.900
the full certificate
chain that it

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:08.577
has to supply up to the server.

00:13:08.577 --> 00:13:11.160
All of that stuff can blow out
the memory of a microcontroller

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:12.850
very, very quickly.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:16.110
So Cloud IoT Core is designed
to solve this problem

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:19.770
by creating an asymmetric
authentication between the two.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:24.480
It still uses TLS 1.2 to
authenticate the server

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:25.570
to the devices.

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.390
So IoT Core still
presents certificates

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:30.670
that can be validated on the
device, and all of that stuff.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:35.280
But the devices authenticate
themselves back to IoT Core

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.530
using a much simpler
public-private key

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:42.270
authentication mechanism,
using JSON Web Tokens or jots.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:45.570
Basically what happens here
is, when an IoT Core device is

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.230
connecting to the
service, it signs a jot

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.960
with a private key that is
stored privately and securely

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:54.780
on that device, and
then provides that jot

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.540
as the password for
the connection to IoT

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:00.300
Core over MQTT or HTDP.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:03.630
The public key is shared
at provisioning time

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.130
with IoT Core.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.860
So it can use that
public key to validate

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.620
that the information coming
in from this device is valid,

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.600
it is the device ID that
it's claiming to be,

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.260
and all of those things.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.740
So we still have a secure
authentication mechanism

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.410
from the client device
back up to the server,

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:22.470
but it's using a much more
lightweight process that

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:26.280
is more easy to fit into these
constrained embedded devices

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:27.750
than a full-blown mutual TLS.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.460
All right, one of the
advantages of using

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:37.240
Firebase authentication for
user identity and access control

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:38.530
is security rules.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.930
Has anyone developed
using Firebase in here?

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:42.370
OK, just a few people.

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:44.050
Firebase is an amazing platform.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:45.520
It is built on
Google Cloud, but it

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.040
adds a bunch of additional
developer services that

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:50.500
make it really, really
integrated, seamless,

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:51.740
and easy to use.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:54.220
And one of those is Firebase
authentication integration

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.840
with Data Store using
the security rules.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.770
So with security rules,
we can seamlessly

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:03.220
integrate user permissions
to access the Firestore data,

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:06.170
using a very simple
declarative rule syntax,

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:07.870
without having to
write any code, right.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:10.720
And this syntax
allows us to validate,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.540
based on the authenticated
users and the information

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:16.630
that we have about them, what
types of data in the database

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:18.370
should they be able
to access, right.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.640
And this is basically a
snapshot of the Firestore rules

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.900
from the [INAUDIBLE]
example that we have,

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.280
which allow us to control that
only authenticated users should

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.632
be able to access device
data, and they should only

00:15:29.632 --> 00:15:31.090
be able to access
the devices where

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:33.670
we have marked them as the
owner of that device, OK.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:35.440
Now, the logistics of
how all that happens

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:36.940
is, you can look
in the sample code.

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:40.030
But the basic idea is, with just
a couple of lines in the rules,

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:42.160
we apply that over our
entire database, OK.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:45.200
So it's very simple, it's very
seamless, it's easy to do.

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.880
We can also restrict not only
user access, but users' ability

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.760
to do things like create
new devices, right,

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.900
so that we can mediate the
process of device registration.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.233
And we'll talk about that a
little bit more in a minute.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:03.100
OK, along with
security rules, one

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.170
of the other primary
advantages of Firebase

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:08.210
is the ability to build client
apps, as I mentioned before.

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:11.680
So this is a screenshot of the
mobile app that comes along

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:12.980
with the end-to-end example.

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:14.813
This happens to have
been written in Flutter

00:16:14.813 --> 00:16:18.100
using the native Flutter SDK
that's available for Firebase.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:21.250
Firebase also has SDKs
for Android and iOS.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:24.550
And on the web, we have
SDKs for React, Angular,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:25.870
and many, many more, right.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.300
So it's a very easy
system to integrate

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:31.850
for front-end applications
both on the web and on mobile.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:34.880
And this enables you
to very quickly create

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:36.970
properly functional
applications to enable

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.150
users to authenticate,
access their devices,

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:43.730
manage them, control, all
of those types of things.

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:45.550
Now, the mobile
application in particular

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.470
is actually quite
important because it's

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:50.500
part of a critical path
for device provisioning.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.425
It allows users to activate
and register their devices.

00:16:54.425 --> 00:16:56.300
So I won't ask for a
show of hands this time,

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.900
but I'll bet that those of you
who mentioned that you have

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:01.540
activated a smart device
in your home before,

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.000
more than likely you had
to download the mobile app

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.800
from that manufacturer
to provision

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:07.880
that device, whether
it was on your network,

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:09.790
or with your account,
or any of that.

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.099
This is a very common
pattern because it's a device

00:17:12.099 --> 00:17:14.410
that we can pretty much
assume that all users will

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:15.730
have with them, right?

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.500
Some devices can
do this on the web.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.670
Most of them use the mobile app.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.569
So let's dive a bit deeper into
the device provisioning process

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:26.210
and how we might do this with
something like Cloud IoT Core.

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.570
So, within IoT Core, I
mentioned before that devices

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.910
use this public-private key
authentication mechanism when

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:34.840
they connect to the service.

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:37.440
So one of the things that
the OEM, or the factory,

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:40.920
is responsible for doing
is producing that key pair

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.090
and ensuring that the public
key is shared with IoT Core

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:47.400
and the private key is
securely stored on that device

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.520
before it's boxed up
and ready to ship.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:54.090
That basically creates the
initial device association

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.770
to say that this
is a valid device,

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.400
this is its ID,
or serial number,

00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:58.860
or whatever you want to provide.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.120
That can be done by
the manufacturer.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:03.930
But then, at some point later,
when the user actually takes

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.930
this thing out of the box, we
have to have a way for them

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.150
to prove that the device
they have in front of them,

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:12.240
that they're trying to register,
is a valid device that we

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:13.920
know about.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.960
One of the ways that
you can do that is you

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.830
can share that same
public key with the user

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.260
so that they can provide
that public key during device

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.600
registration and allow
us, on the back end,

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:27.450
to validate that the
public key matches

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:31.193
the one that's in IoT Core for
that particular device ID, OK?

00:18:31.193 --> 00:18:32.610
There's a number
of ways you could

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:35.400
do that, in terms of how
that information is shared.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.130
One example, which is how
we do it in the example code

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:40.260
that I mentioned
to you earlier, is

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:43.770
you can bind that public
key to something physical

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.855
on the device, like a QR code.

00:18:45.855 --> 00:18:47.730
And this is a very common
provisioning method

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:50.373
that's used by a lot
of Smart Home vendors.

00:18:50.373 --> 00:18:52.290
One of the nice features
of this functionality

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.920
is that it actually enhances the
security of the device as well,

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.330
over doing provisioning methods
wirelessly like BLE or MDNS,

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:01.390
or some of those others.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:02.820
Not that there's anything
wrong with those,

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.920
but they require additional
layers of security

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.462
to make sure you can prove that
that device is the one you're

00:19:07.462 --> 00:19:08.760
talking to, right?

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:11.770
When you physically bind
this data to the device,

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:13.260
you can guarantee
that the user has

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:17.190
to have physical access to the
device in order to register it.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:19.170
So you can bind all of
that data-- maybe you

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.360
want some additional metadata
like the device serial

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.970
number or anything else-- but
the public key in this example

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:25.650
is the one that's
most important.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.830
You can encode all of that
onto the device using something

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:31.420
like a QR code.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.630
And then, because all of our
mobile devices have cameras,

00:19:34.630 --> 00:19:36.160
you can use the
mobile application

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:39.910
to scan that QR code, gather
that information easily--

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:41.770
not asking the
user to type it in

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:44.500
or anything like that--
they just scan the QR code,

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:47.020
submit that device
information up to Firestore,

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:48.940
and then our back
end service can then

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.510
validate that information.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.260
So the user basically
submits a request

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:56.410
to register this device
with a particular device

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:58.120
ID and public key.

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:01.390
And then in the back end, we
can validate with IoT Core,

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:04.512
oh yes, that device ID does
exist, here's its public key.

00:20:04.512 --> 00:20:06.970
And that actually matches what
the user has just given you.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:10.020
OK, so we know, to a
reasonable degree of certainty,

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:12.270
that the user actually has
the device in front of them

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.090
that they're trying to register.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:17.110
And then from there, we can
create the actual device node,

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.820
assign that user
ID as the owner,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:21.760
and then the user can see it
in their mobile app on the web,

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.320
and all of that.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:28.720
OK, so that's kind of a quick
overview of the example code.

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:30.520
I encourage you to take
a look at the code.

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:32.145
We also have a couple
of blog posts out

00:20:32.145 --> 00:20:34.360
on that that are linked
from the repository,

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:35.943
as well, to kind of
give you some more

00:20:35.943 --> 00:20:39.570
detail on the nitty gritty of
how all that was put together.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.180
So let's shift gears
a little bit, now

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.040
and talk a little
bit more about,

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:45.230
now that we have a
Cloud Service built,

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:47.330
what does it mean to
actually integrate

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.400
that service with
the Google Assistant?

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:52.370
How do we get the interactions
that I just showed you before,

00:20:52.370 --> 00:20:57.290
with a Smart Display or a Smart
Speaker or devices like that?

00:20:57.290 --> 00:21:00.110
So this diagram kind
of outlines the basics

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.120
of how the Google
Assistant expects

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.160
to interact with
your Cloud service

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.488
when you're integrating
the smart home API.

00:21:06.488 --> 00:21:08.030
Integrating with
Smart Home basically

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.640
requires you, as a developer,
to implement the pieces

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.610
up here that are in
yellow, which boils down

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:15.630
to two primary elements.

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.300
The first is account
linking, which

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:19.730
is represented by
that web sign in flow

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:21.750
that you see up
there at the top.

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.793
And the second is
intent fulfillment,

00:21:23.793 --> 00:21:25.460
which you see between
the cloud services

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:26.760
there in the Assistant.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.177
So we're going to talk about
both of these in a little bit

00:21:29.177 --> 00:21:31.620
more detail, here, starting
with the account linking.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:34.700
So account linking is
a process initiated

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:39.170
from within the Google Home app
that enables users to authorize

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:40.925
access to those devices.

00:21:40.925 --> 00:21:42.800
So some of you have
probably experienced this

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.110
before, but to just kind
of talk through the idea--

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.630
if a user wants to
enable their Smart Home

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:50.210
device through the Assistant,
they go through the Google Home

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:53.230
app, they decide they
want to add a new device,

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:55.730
and they click that they want
to link their existing account

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:59.300
from some other vendor
like Nest, or whoever,

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:00.650
to the Google Assistant.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.800
That triggers a sign in flow
through this account linking

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.400
that enables them to log in
using the account that they had

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:11.000
with that partner device, and
then authorize that the Google

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.720
Assistant can have access to all
of the devices that are managed

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.547
by that particular account.

00:22:16.547 --> 00:22:18.380
What you're seeing here
is just a screenshot

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:20.150
of the account
linking implementation

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:21.092
that we have provided.

00:22:21.092 --> 00:22:23.300
And again, that end-to-end
example, that's on GitHub.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:28.080
So Smart Home account
linking really boils

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.550
down to three primary elements.

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:33.330
Now, for those of you who
are familiar with OAuth 2.0,

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:34.830
this is going to
look very familiar.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:37.590
Because basically what we
require of your service

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:41.940
is to be able to expose to us an
OAuth 2.0 compatible endpoint,

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.860
so that we can do an
authorization code

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.220
flow with your cloud service.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.840
OK, so that basically
means we need

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.240
to be able to handle the
authorization step, which

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.810
is the user signing in and
granting access, and then

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:57.810
later on in the process
providing access credentials

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.660
and refreshed credentials,
which again is a typical OAuth

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.950
flow for those things.

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:09.240
So in the example, if
you were to go and look

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:10.830
through the example
code on GitHub,

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.600
we've implemented this
in the following way.

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.900
So the authorization
step, we interact

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:17.510
with Firebase
authentication, which

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.385
is really nice because
it means we don't have

00:23:19.385 --> 00:23:21.000
to write much code to do this.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:24.750
Firebase authentication handles
this part of the authorization

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:25.800
flow very well.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:28.380
The user can sign in
using the same account,

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:31.680
and then we can use that
user's ID token, provided to us

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:34.260
by Firebase, as the
authorization code

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:36.120
that we can provide
back to Google, OK.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.050
Very little code
involved in this part.

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:42.080
Then, later, Google will pass
that authorization code back

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:44.630
to our Cloud service,
asking us for what's

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:46.200
known as a refresh token.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.020
This is basically
a persistent token

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:51.260
that should be valid as long
as the user's account is

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:53.350
linked to the Assistant, OK.

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:55.100
So this is something
that we, on our side,

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:57.350
need to generate,
return, and then

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:58.727
we persist it in Firestore.

00:23:58.727 --> 00:24:01.310
Now, in the example, if you look
through the source code, what

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:03.710
you'll see is that we
actually use the same jot

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:06.390
standard to create the
tokens for this purpose

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.620
that IoT Core uses to
authenticate devices.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:13.440
It's the same standard, in case
you're looking at the code.

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:15.150
Once we've created
this refresh token,

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:16.920
we can then return that back.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:20.280
This is a token
that now authorizes

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:24.210
Google to periodically
request access credentials

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.760
from our service,
known as access tokens.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:29.640
So this refresh token will be
passed back to your service

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:33.300
during and after the account
linking process, periodically,

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:35.130
to get what's known
as an access token.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:36.750
Access tokens are short lived.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.000
They expire at some time
in the future, which

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.250
is why this process
happens periodically so

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:43.140
that, when that
token expires, you

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:46.530
can give Google a new one,
and so on and so forth.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:49.230
So similarly, when we
get this refresh token,

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:51.210
it's our responsibility
to validate

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:54.000
that the token we received
matches the one we persisted

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.530
for this particular user.

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:58.270
If it's not, it's
an invalid token.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:00.130
And then, if that
does match, then we

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:02.110
can provide an access token.

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:05.790
And then, later on, that will
be used in future requests

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.290
for intent fulfillment-- that
we'll talk about in a minute--

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:11.080
for accessing actual
device data, OK.

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:13.880
The last piece to account
linking is at some point,

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:16.750
the user may decide to
unlink their account

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:18.070
from the Assistant.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:21.280
When that happens, you'll
receive a disconnect message

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:24.130
and your Cloud service
basically, in our example,

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:26.890
just needs to destroy the
token that we considered valid,

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.228
as a refresh.

00:25:28.228 --> 00:25:30.520
There are a number of different
ways to implement this.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:32.290
And in a lot of
cases you can even

00:25:32.290 --> 00:25:35.440
use third party services that
support OAuth, like Auth Zero,

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:37.713
or maybe you've already got
this in your application.

00:25:37.713 --> 00:25:39.130
But this kind of
gives you an idea

00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:40.887
of how we built it
in the example code,

00:25:40.887 --> 00:25:42.970
in case you want to see
what this might look like,

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:44.178
implementing it for yourself.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:50.340
All right, let's move a little
bit now to intent fulfillment.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:54.660
So Smart Home Intents enable
users to access and control

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:57.520
the devices that they've linked
through voice interaction,

00:25:57.520 --> 00:25:59.520
as well as through the
Google Home UI, Remember,

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:00.937
I kind of showed
you both of those

00:26:00.937 --> 00:26:03.740
as we were going
through the demo.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:06.200
Smart Home Intents,
the fulfillment

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:09.510
basically requires that you
handle three primary intents,

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:14.420
a sync intent, a query
intent, and an execute intent.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:17.530
So the sync intent basically
provides information

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:21.100
to the Assistant about what
devices does this user control.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:24.160
The query intent allows you
to return the current state

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:25.600
of a particular device.

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:27.400
And the execute
intent is what you'll

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:30.628
use to modify the state
of a particular device.

00:26:30.628 --> 00:26:32.920
OK, so let's look at these
in a little bit more detail.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:37.240
Here we go.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:39.930
So the sync intent
is sent automatically

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.070
after account
linking is complete.

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:43.740
So this is the
Assistant's attempt

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:45.990
to ask your Cloud
service, what are

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:49.290
all the devices that this
authenticated user controls,

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:52.230
and what can those devices do?

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:54.570
So your job is
basically to respond

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:57.750
to this intent by providing
the metadata about all

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.560
of the device types and the
device traits associated

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:03.810
with the devices under
that user's account.

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:08.820
So in this example, we might
return a response that says,

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:11.640
this user has four light
devices in their account.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.220
All four of them can
report the on-off trait,

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:16.740
but only two of them
support brightness, right,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.340
something along those lines.

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:21.270
So this is the kind of metadata
that you provide in response

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:22.860
to a sink.

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:24.960
Now generally
speaking, syncs are not

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:27.180
performed on a regular basis.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:29.580
By default, a sync
is only performed

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.640
when the account is linked, OK.

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:34.770
And this can be problematic
if this is the only thing

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:36.780
that your service
implements because it means

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:38.880
that if the user
adds a new device,

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:40.770
they have to actually
unlink their account

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:44.220
and relink it again
to see the new device.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.500
Now we mitigate
this by enabling you

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.390
to call a method
through the Home Graph

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.560
API called request sync.

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:52.530
So if, inside of
your application,

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:56.070
users add new devices or
remove devices, or maybe just

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.440
the devices that are
there change capabilities

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:00.990
in some way, you can
use the Home Graph

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:03.960
API to tell the assistant
to request a new sync.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:05.350
Something has changed.

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:07.020
We need you to
send a sync again.

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:09.900
And when that happens, you'll
get the same sync intent

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:12.630
so that you can respond
with the new set of data,

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:14.640
the new set of devices
and capabilities,

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:17.460
associated with that
particular user.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:19.380
So request sync
is very important

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:23.550
because it enables users to
have a seamless experience

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.550
between the Google Assistant
and your application,

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:30.060
and not forcing them to manually
resync or relink their account

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:31.800
or anything like that
just because they

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:34.170
bought a new device,
right, and applied it

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:37.260
to an existing account.

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.060
All right, so the query intent.

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:40.800
Query is responsible
for reporting

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:43.650
the current state of
an individual device

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:46.400
or a set of devices.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:49.100
In this case, the
Assistant is asking you

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.300
for some more specific
information about, what

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:54.020
is this device currently doing?

00:28:54.020 --> 00:28:55.610
Is the light currently on?

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:59.330
Is the thermostat set to 20
degrees or 25 degrees, right?

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:01.340
So in this case,
your response is

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:05.780
going to provide back the actual
values for all of the device

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:08.090
traits and attributes,
that you provided,

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:11.670
that this device was able to
report inside of the sync.

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:15.140
This includes whether or not
the device is online or offline,

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:18.690
if you're tracking
that kind of status.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:21.180
The query intent is
typically triggered

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:24.450
when the user navigates
to a particular device

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:26.440
from the Google Home app.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:30.000
Or maybe through voice when
they say something like, what

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.740
is the temperature in here?

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:33.850
As soon as that
request is triggered,

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:37.200
then this intent is going to
be sent to your Cloud service,

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.230
and you need to send that
back with the result.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:43.440
Now, both the sync intent
and the query intent,

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:46.140
in the end-to-end example
that we've been discussing,

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:49.350
both of those only have
to go so far as Firebase.

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.870
Because in the architecture,
that we've created

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:54.960
Firestore is the source of
truth for all of this data.

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:58.140
So I can get all the information
I need about what devices

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:00.240
a user owns, what
they can do, and what

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:01.890
the current state
of those devices

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:04.200
is, simply by
talking to Firebase.

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:06.152
I don't have to
actually go all the way

00:30:06.152 --> 00:30:07.860
and do a round trip
to the devices, which

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:09.900
is very important
because it enables

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:13.470
those queries coming back from
voice to be instantaneous.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:15.330
You're not seeing
this long latency

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:17.970
while we actually block waiting
for something to go all the way

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:20.160
down to the device and back.

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:22.680
So this enables a much
quicker interaction

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:25.690
with the Google Assistant.

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:30.010
The execute intent
enables you to modify

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:31.690
the state of your
particular devices

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:33.220
based on a user command.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:35.380
So this basically
comes in and describes

00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:38.290
a set of traits that
should be updated

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:41.020
for a given device or
a given set of devices.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:42.890
And these can be
provided in a group.

00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:44.590
So you can get an
execute intent that

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:48.040
says you should modify on-off
and brightness for these three

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.520
lights, and then you do all
of that in a single set.

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:53.890
That basically is
what allows us to be

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.530
able to make some of
those more complex queries

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:58.840
like, turn on the
lights upstairs.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.990
There could be six lights
from three different vendors

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:04.540
in that particular
group, and this

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:06.130
enables us to make
sure that that all

00:31:06.130 --> 00:31:09.750
happens in a single command.

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:11.693
The response from
an execute intent

00:31:11.693 --> 00:31:13.860
is a little bit different
because we're not actually

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:16.793
telling the Assistant any more
information about the devices.

00:31:16.793 --> 00:31:18.210
We just need to
tell the Assistant

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:20.230
whether that succeeded or not.

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:22.770
So you can tell it
for any particular one

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:25.770
of those devices, did
the command succeed?

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:27.470
And succeed really
means that you

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:30.780
know it got down to the device,
the device made that change,

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:33.840
and reported that it was
successful back to you.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:35.670
So this is a case where
you might actually

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:38.040
be talking to Cloud
IoT Core directly,

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:40.140
depending on your application.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:42.280
In the sample, we
don't actually do that.

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:46.260
We modify the state of these
devices through Firestore.

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:49.590
And because of that, the
responses back from the device,

00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:52.540
that a change has actually
happened, is asynchronous.

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:55.650
So in our example, we actually
return a different state

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:57.630
than success, which is pending.

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:00.520
Pending tells the
Assistant that, yes,

00:32:00.520 --> 00:32:02.400
a command is
expected to succeed,

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:04.530
but we haven't seen it yet.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:07.410
You can also tell the Assistant
if you know, for some reason,

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.758
that that command failed
because of an error,

00:32:09.758 --> 00:32:11.550
or maybe because the
device isn't reachable

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:13.410
because it's not online.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:15.750
So all that information
can be reported back

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:19.230
as a result of an execute intent
for each individual device.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.260
And then as you might expect,
this is the type of intent

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:25.020
that your cloud service will
see when the user walks up

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:27.600
to this device and says
something like, set

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.730
the temperature to 52 degrees.

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:32.130
That's going to be packaged
up as an execute intent

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:36.040
and then sent down to
your Cloud service.

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:38.920
OK, now one additional
piece of information here

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:40.990
that's relatively
important to implement--

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:44.080
it's not required per se,
but provides a much better

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:45.957
experience similar
to request sync--

00:32:45.957 --> 00:32:48.040
is there's another API
call through the Home Graph

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:49.990
that we call report state.

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:52.678
If the device changes state
locally, for some reason,

00:32:52.678 --> 00:32:54.970
maybe it's a light switch
and they actually walked over

00:32:54.970 --> 00:32:56.095
and flipped on the switch--

00:32:56.095 --> 00:32:59.560
I know it's weird to think of,
but it actually does happen--

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:02.930
or maybe they changed that
state outside of the Assistant,

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:03.430
right.

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:06.520
Maybe in your client
application or through the web

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:08.660
they modified the
state of that device.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:10.150
This is something
where that state

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:13.900
change isn't known automatically
by the Google Assistant.

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:16.540
And by default, this
is not a huge problem,

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:19.240
because whenever the user
wants to ask something,

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:21.370
you'll generally
initiate a query anyway.

00:33:21.370 --> 00:33:23.390
But queries have
latency to them.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:26.140
So what you can do to improve
this experience is implementing

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:29.650
the report state API where, any
time one of those local state

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:31.660
changes happens,
that you're aware of,

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:34.630
you can report that state
change to the Assistant

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:37.660
by providing the exact
traits and values that

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:40.120
changed for any given device.

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:43.300
This can drastically reduce
the number of query intents

00:33:43.300 --> 00:33:46.300
that your service sees, because
if there are recent state

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:48.850
changes on a device, Google
Assistant can actually

00:33:48.850 --> 00:33:52.390
look those up locally without
having to ask you again.

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:56.140
In addition, in cases
where, like in our example

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:58.930
that I mentioned before, where
state changes in response

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:01.120
to commands might
be asynchronous.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:03.580
So you can't actually
tell, in response

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:05.800
to an execute, whether
the command succeeded

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:07.870
and so you return
pending instead.

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:10.540
Report state is the way
that you can basically

00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:14.020
tell the Assistant, OK, that
command has actually changed.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:15.880
Because it happened in
an asynchronous way,

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:19.840
you can call this API, this is
the new state of the device.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:21.340
So report state
is really helpful

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:24.070
for confirming those
successive pending commands

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:28.179
and in reducing the latency
between those potential queries

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:33.550
by notifying the Assistant of
those changes as they happen.

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:36.070
OK, that's pretty much it.

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:38.429
So, just to quickly
summarize, we

00:34:38.429 --> 00:34:40.860
talked about Cloud
IoT Core as a way

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:44.870
to securely connect constrained
devices to Google Cloud.

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:47.600
We added Firebase as
a way to easily build

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.989
those authenticated client
apps to give users access

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:52.830
to those devices.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:54.949
And then we included the
Google Assistant as a way

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:57.020
to provide intelligent
interactions

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:58.880
with those smart home devices.

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:02.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

